   The drum didn’t stop. “And the girl on the shekere?”
   “That’s Yenni. She’s breathing in the gaps.”
   “And Manny?”
   “Manny’s two steps ahead of everyone and pretending he isn’t.”
   A ripple of laughter went around the ring. Manny said, “I resent
that,” and played a note to prove it.
   “Forty years,” Rafa said, to nobody and everybody. “It took me forty
years to hear a circle like that.” He didn’t say and you did it in four
minutes. He didn’t have to. He reached behind him and tossed me
something underhand, a small gourd shaker wrapped in beaded net‐
ting, worn smooth by a lot of hands.
   “Don’t keep time,” he said. “You’ll be terrible at keeping time. Your
whole self fights a straight line. I can see it from here.” A few people
laughed, kindly, with me and not at me. “Don’t keep time. Answer.
Listen to the whole circle and put the shaker where it’s got a hole.”



I was terrible for about ninety seconds.
    Of course I tried to keep time. He’d told me not to, and I’m me. I
clattered against the beat, a half-step behind everybody, and I felt the
old collapse starting, a trapdoor opening under me, see, you can’t even
do this one thing, everyone else can.
    Then the cowbell kid looked at me across the ring and grinned. And
I stopped keeping time, and I just listened for a hole.
    There was one. Right there, a breath wide, between the bucket and
the güira, a place that the circle had been leaving empty for a bar and a
half like a chair at a table. I put the shaker in it. I played around the
beat instead of on it, in the web instead of on the line, the way my head
has always worked.
    And the circle opened to let me in.
    I felt it. Fifteen people adjusted by a hair to make room for what I
was doing, and what I was doing fit. The scatter fit. The forty tabs fit.




